            <ArticleTitle>Inhibitory effect of the paraoxonase gene on the formation of rabbit coronary atherosclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>544-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/S1995-7645(13)60093-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1995-7645(13)60093-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To observe the effect on the inhibition of coronary atherosclerosis hardening of the paraoxonase gene (PON-1) which transfected to the rabbit epicardial adipose tissue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rabbit coronary atherosclerosis model was established by high-fat feeding, liposome-encapsulated recombinant plasmid pEGFP-PON-1 50 μ L was injected to the rabbit pericardial cavity, and was harvested 4 weeks after transfection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The epicardial fat transfected PON-1 gene had effect on the high lipid level. It significantly increased expression of PON-1 in peripheral arterial vascular tissue (P &lt;0.05); and significantly reduced total cholesterol and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels (P&lt;0.05), and the thickness ratio of coronary artery intima/media (P &lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The injection of the PON-1 gene in the pericardial cavity can effectively suppress the formation of coronary atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
